 questions:mix([
  q(DP,'What does the term “etiology” refer to?','The cause of a disease',['The expected outcome of a disease','The signs found on physical exam','The number of new cases each year'],'A','Etiology is the cause or origin of a disease; prognosis is the expected outcome and incidence counts new cases.',FRA),
  q(DP,'Which is a sign rather than a symptom?','A measured fever of 102 °F',['A headache the patient describes','Nausea the patient reports feeling','Fatigue the patient says they have'],'B','Signs are objective findings others can observe or measure; symptoms are subjective experiences only the patient can report.',FRA),
  q(DP,'A patient receives mismatched blood, and antibodies destroy the donor red cells. Which hypersensitivity type is this?','Type II, antibody-mediated cytotoxic',['Type I, IgE-mediated immediate','Type III, immune complex deposition','Type IV, delayed T-cell mediated'],'C','In type II reactions, antibodies bind antigens on cell surfaces and destroy those cells, as in transfusion reactions.',ZEL),
  q(DP,'Brain tissue that dies after a stroke softens into a liquid mass. Which type of necrosis is this?','Liquefactive necrosis',['Coagulative necrosis','Caseous necrosis','Fat necrosis'],'D','Liquefactive necrosis turns dead tissue into fluid and is typical of the brain and of abscesses; coagulative necrosis keeps tissue structure.',ZEL),
  q(DP,'Abnormal changes in the size, shape and organization of cells that may become cancer are called:','Dysplasia',['Atrophy','Hypertrophy','Hyperplasia'],'A','Dysplasia is disordered cell growth that is often precancerous, as found on an abnormal Pap test.',FRA),
  q(DP,'How does tumor grading differ from staging?','Grading describes how abnormal the cells look; staging describes spread',['Grading describes spread; staging describes how cells look under a microscope','Grading and staging both measure only the tumor’s size in centimeters','Grading is used only for benign tumors and staging only for blood cancers'],'B','Grade reflects how differentiated the cells are; stage (such as TNM) reflects tumor size, node involvement and metastasis.',ZEL),
  q(INTEG,'A patient has well-defined white patches where skin pigment has been lost. What causes this condition?','Autoimmune destruction of melanocytes',['A fungal infection of the outer skin','Overgrowth of collagen after injury','A bacterial infection of hair follicles'],'C','Vitiligo results from loss of melanocytes, often autoimmune, leaving depigmented patches.',FRA),
  q(INTEG,'An adult has facial flushing, redness across the cheeks and nose, and small visible blood vessels. What is the likely diagnosis?','Rosacea',['Acne vulgaris','Lupus rash only','Contact dermatitis'],'D','Rosacea is a chronic inflammatory condition of the central face with flushing, persistent redness and telangiectasias.',FRA),
  q(INTEG,'Which patient is at highest risk for a pressure injury?','A bedridden patient who cannot change position',['A runner who trains outdoors every day','A teen with mild acne on the forehead','An adult with seasonal hay fever'],'A','Prolonged pressure over bony areas cuts off blood flow; immobility, poor nutrition and moisture raise the risk.',ZEL),
  q(INTEG,'A rough, scaly patch on sun-damaged skin is considered precancerous. What is it called?','Actinic keratosis',['Seborrheic keratosis','Lipoma','Dermatofibroma'],'B','Actinic keratoses form from chronic sun exposure and can progress to squamous cell carcinoma; seborrheic keratoses are benign.',FRA),
  q(MSK,'A patient has widespread muscle pain, tender points, fatigue and poor sleep, with normal lab tests. What is the likely diagnosis?','Fibromyalgia',['Polymyositis','Rheumatoid arthritis','Osteomyelitis'],'C','Fibromyalgia causes chronic widespread pain and tenderness linked to altered pain processing, without inflammation or joint damage.',FRA),
  q(MSK,'In adults, softening of bones from vitamin D deficiency is called:','Osteomalacia',['Osteoporosis','Osteosarcoma','Osteoarthritis'],'D','Osteomalacia is poor mineralization of bone in adults; the same deficiency in children causes rickets.',FRA),
  q(MSK,'A screening exam shows a teen’s spine curving sideways in an S shape. What is this condition?','Scoliosis',['Kyphosis','Lordosis','Spondylosis'],'A','Scoliosis is a lateral curvature of the spine, often found in adolescence; kyphosis is a hunchback curve and lordosis a swayback.',FRA),
  q(MSK,'A runner has sharp heel pain with the first steps each morning. What is the likely cause?','Plantar fasciitis',['Achilles tendon rupture','Gout of the big toe','Stress fracture of the hip'],'B','Plantar fasciitis is inflammation of the thick tissue on the sole; pain is worst with the first steps after rest.',FRA),
  q(MSK,'A smooth, round, fluid-filled lump appears over a wrist tendon. What is it most likely?','A ganglion cyst',['An osteosarcoma','A gouty tophus','A fractured bone'],'C','Ganglion cysts are benign, jelly-filled sacs that arise from joint capsules or tendon sheaths, often on the wrist.',FRA),
  q(CIRC,'What is the main process in atherosclerosis?','Fatty plaques build up inside artery walls',['Veins dilate because their valves fail','Red blood cells become sickle-shaped','Lymph vessels become blocked by parasites'],'D','Atherosclerosis begins with injury to the artery lining and buildup of LDL cholesterol, forming plaques that narrow vessels.',ZEL),
  q(CIRC,'A patient’s bone marrow stops making enough red cells, white cells and platelets. What is this condition?','Aplastic anemia',['Iron deficiency anemia','Pernicious anemia','Sickle cell anemia'],'A','Aplastic anemia is bone marrow failure causing low counts of all blood cells (pancytopenia).',FRA),
  q(CIRC,'A bone marrow disorder causes overproduction of red blood cells, thickening the blood. What is it called?','Polycythemia vera',['Hemophilia A','Thalassemia major','Aplastic anemia'],'B','Polycythemia vera makes too many red cells, increasing blood thickness and the risk of clots.',FRA),
  q(CIRC,'An inherited disorder reduces the production of hemoglobin chains, causing small red cells. What is this?','Thalassemia',['Hemophilia','Leukemia','Polycythemia'],'C','Thalassemias are inherited defects in making alpha or beta globin chains, causing microcytic anemia; they are common in Mediterranean and Asian ancestry.',FRA),
  q(CIRC,'A patient has sharp chest pain that improves when leaning forward, and a friction rub. Which structure is inflamed?','The pericardium',['The myocardium','The endocardium','The aorta'],'D','Pericarditis inflames the sac around the heart; pain eases when leaning forward, and a scratchy friction rub may be heard.',ZEL),
  q(CIRC,'In septic shock, what mainly causes the low blood pressure?','Widespread dilation of blood vessels from infection',['Loss of blood from a large wound','Failure of the heart muscle to pump','An allergic reaction to a bee sting'],'A','Infection triggers inflammatory chemicals that widen vessels and make them leaky, so blood pressure falls despite normal blood volume.',ZEL),
  q(RESP,'How is tuberculosis usually spread?','Through airborne droplets from coughing',['Through contaminated food and water','Through mosquito and tick bites','Through skin contact with sores'],'B','Mycobacterium tuberculosis spreads by airborne droplet nuclei when an infected person coughs, sneezes or talks.',FRA),
  q(RESP,'Which virus most commonly causes bronchiolitis in infants?','Respiratory syncytial virus (RSV)',['Epstein-Barr virus (EBV)','Human papillomavirus (HPV)','Hepatitis B virus (HBV)'],'C','RSV inflames the small airways of infants, causing wheezing and trouble breathing, especially in winter.',FRA),
  q(RESP,'After surgery, a patient who breathes shallowly develops collapse of part of the lung. What is this called?','Atelectasis',['Emphysema','Bronchiectasis','Pneumoconiosis'],'D','Atelectasis is collapse of alveoli; shallow breathing and blocked airways after surgery are common causes.',FRA),
  q(RESP,'Fluid collects in the space between the lung and chest wall. What is this called?','Pleural effusion',['Pulmonary edema','Pneumothorax','Pleurisy only'],'A','A pleural effusion is fluid in the pleural space, often from heart failure, infection or cancer, causing shortness of breath.',FRA),
  q(DIG,'The lower esophageal sphincter fails to relax, so food has trouble passing into the stomach. What is this condition?','Achalasia',['Hiatal hernia','Esophageal varices','Pyloric stenosis'],'B','Achalasia is a motility disorder in which nerve loss keeps the sphincter tight, causing difficulty swallowing solids and liquids.',ZEL),
  q(DIG,'A newborn fails to pass stool, and a biopsy shows missing nerve cells in part of the colon. What is the diagnosis?','Hirschsprung disease',['Intussusception','Pyloric stenosis','Celiac disease'],'C','In Hirschsprung disease, a segment of colon lacks ganglion cells and cannot push stool through, causing obstruction.',ZEL),
  q(DIG,'After a course of antibiotics, a patient develops severe watery diarrhea from toxin-producing bacteria. Which organism is likely?','Clostridioides difficile',['Helicobacter pylori','Hepatitis A virus','Escherichia coli in the bladder'],'D','Antibiotics disrupt normal gut flora, allowing C. difficile to overgrow and release toxins that inflame the colon.',FRA),
  q(DIG,'Long-term acid reflux changes the lining of the lower esophagus to intestine-like cells. What is this condition?','Barrett esophagus',['Achalasia','Esophageal varices','Zenker diverticulum'],'A','Barrett esophagus is metaplasia caused by chronic reflux, and it increases the risk of esophageal adenocarcinoma.',ZEL),
  q(DIG,'An inherited disorder causes the body to absorb too much iron, which builds up in the liver and other organs. What is it?','Hemochromatosis',['Wilson disease','Gilbert syndrome','Celiac disease'],'B','Hereditary hemochromatosis causes iron overload that can damage the liver, heart and pancreas; Wilson disease involves copper.',ZEL),
  q(DIG,'A ruptured appendix spreads infection through the abdominal lining. What is this complication called?','Peritonitis',['Gastritis','Pancreatitis','Diverticulosis'],'C','Peritonitis is inflammation of the peritoneum, often from a perforated organ, causing severe pain and a rigid abdomen.',FRA),
  q(ENDO,'Which autoimmune disease is the most common cause of hypothyroidism in the United States?','Hashimoto thyroiditis',['Graves disease','Addison disease','Cushing disease'],'D','In Hashimoto thyroiditis, antibodies attack the thyroid, gradually reducing hormone production.',FRA),
  q(ENDO,'In diabetic ketoacidosis, why do ketones build up in the blood?','Without insulin, the body breaks down fat for energy',['Too much insulin pushes sugar into the cells','The kidneys stop filtering all sugar from blood','The liver stops making any glucose at all'],'A','Lack of insulin keeps glucose out of cells, so the body burns fat and produces acidic ketones, mostly in type 1 diabetes.',ZEL),
  q(ENDO,'In some parts of the world, many people have an enlarged thyroid from a dietary deficiency. Which nutrient is lacking?','Iodine',['Calcium','Vitamin C','Iron'],'B','The thyroid needs iodine to make hormone; without it, TSH stimulates the gland to enlarge, forming a goiter.',FRA),
  q(ENDO,'An adrenal tumor makes too much aldosterone. Which findings are expected?','High blood pressure and low potassium',['Low blood pressure and high potassium','High blood sugar and weight loss','Low calcium and muscle spasms'],'C','Excess aldosterone (Conn syndrome) retains sodium and water and wastes potassium, causing hypertension and hypokalemia.',ZEL),
  q(NERV,'What is the most common type of stroke?','Ischemic stroke from a blocked artery',['Hemorrhagic stroke from a burst artery','Stroke caused by a brain tumor','Stroke caused by low blood sugar'],'D','About 85% of strokes are ischemic, caused by a clot or narrowing that blocks blood flow to part of the brain.',FRA),
  q(NERV,'Stroke-like symptoms resolve completely within an hour and leave no lasting damage. What is this called?','A transient ischemic attack (TIA)',['A completed ischemic stroke','A subarachnoid hemorrhage','A febrile seizure'],'A','A TIA is a brief interruption of blood flow without permanent damage; it warns of a high risk of stroke.',FRA),
  q(NERV,'A patient has sudden, severe, electric-shock pain on one side of the face when chewing. Which cranial nerve is involved?','Trigeminal nerve (V)',['Facial nerve (VII)','Optic nerve (II)','Vagus nerve (X)'],'B','Trigeminal neuralgia affects cranial nerve V, which carries sensation from the face, causing brief stabbing pain.',ZEL),
  q(NERV,'An older adult gradually loses the ability to hear high-pitched sounds in both ears. What is this called?','Presbycusis',['Otitis media','Otosclerosis only','Ménière disease'],'C','Presbycusis is age-related sensorineural hearing loss from wear on the inner ear hair cells, starting with high frequencies.',FRA),
  q(NERV,'A child’s eyes do not line up, with one turning inward. What is this condition?','Strabismus',['Glaucoma','Cataract','Nystagmus'],'D','Strabismus is misalignment of the eyes from muscle imbalance; untreated, it can lead to amblyopia (lazy eye).',FRA),
  q(REPRO,'Testicular cancer is most common in which age group?','Young men about 15 to 35 years old',['Men over 75 years old','Boys under 5 years old','Men aged about 55 to 70 years old'],'A','Testicular cancer is the most common cancer in young men; a painless lump is a typical first sign.',FRA),
  q(REPRO,'Which virus most commonly causes recurring painful genital blisters?','Herpes simplex virus type 2',['Human immunodeficiency virus','Hepatitis A virus','Varicella-zoster virus'],'B','HSV-2 causes most genital herpes, which stays dormant in nerves and can reactivate as painful blisters.',FRA),
  q(REPRO,'A pregnant patient has painless, bright red vaginal bleeding late in pregnancy. Which condition is suspected?','Placenta previa, with the placenta covering the cervix',['Ectopic pregnancy in the fallopian tube','Preeclampsia with high blood pressure','Gestational diabetes with high sugar'],'C','In placenta previa, the placenta implants over or near the cervix and can bleed painlessly in the third trimester.',ZEL),
  q(URIN,'A kidney stone blocks the ureter, and the kidney swells with trapped urine. What is this called?','Hydronephrosis',['Nephrotic syndrome','Polycystic kidney disease','Glomerulonephritis'],'D','Hydronephrosis is dilation of the renal pelvis and calyces from blocked urine flow, which can damage the kidney.',FRA),
  q(URIN,'Kidney tubule cells are damaged by toxins or prolonged low blood flow. What type of kidney injury is this?','Acute tubular necrosis, an intrarenal injury',['Prerenal injury from dehydration only','Postrenal injury from a blocked urethra','Chronic kidney disease from diabetes'],'A','Acute tubular necrosis is the most common intrarenal cause of acute kidney injury, from ischemia or toxic drugs.',ZEL),
  q(URIN,'A child has repeated kidney infections because urine flows backward from the bladder into the ureters. What is this?','Vesicoureteral reflux',['Urinary incontinence','Interstitial cystitis','Wilms tumor'],'B','Vesicoureteral reflux lets urine flow back toward the kidneys, raising the risk of pyelonephritis and scarring in children.',ZEL),
  q(MENT,'A teen of normal weight repeatedly binge-eats, then vomits or uses laxatives to prevent weight gain. What is the diagnosis?','Bulimia nervosa',['Anorexia nervosa','Binge-eating disorder','Pica'],'C','Bulimia involves binge eating followed by compensatory behaviors, often at a normal weight; binge-eating disorder lacks purging.',FRA),
  q(MENT,'Chronic depressed mood lasting most days for at least 2 years in adults describes which disorder?','Persistent depressive disorder (dysthymia)',['Major depressive disorder, single episode','Seasonal affective disorder','Bipolar I disorder'],'D','Persistent depressive disorder is a long-lasting, often milder depression lasting at least 2 years in adults (1 year in children).',FRA),
  q(MENT,'A student has intense fear of being judged and avoids speaking in class or eating in public. What is the likely diagnosis?','Social anxiety disorder',['Agoraphobia','Panic disorder','Autism spectrum disorder'],'A','Social anxiety disorder is marked fear of social situations where one may be scrutinized, leading to avoidance.',FRA),
  q(MENT,'A person who uses a substance heavily has shaking, sweating and anxiety when they stop. What is this called?','Withdrawal',['Tolerance','Remission','Intoxication'],'B','Withdrawal is the set of symptoms that occur when a dependent person stops or cuts down a substance; tolerance is needing more for the same effect.',FRA)
 ],9503)
